             <span class="problem-title">E2 · The expected t at the planted effect</span>
             <p>Using Proposition 2.1 and the monthly spread standard deviation implied by \(\sigma = 0.08\) and 100 firms per leg, compute the expected \(t\) at \(\beta = -0.0008\) and \(T = 120\). Compare with the median of 2.155 in Lab 1.</p>
             <details class="solution"><summary>Solution</summary>
-              <p>Each leg averages 100 firms, so the variance of a leg average is \(\sigma^2/100\), and the spread of the two legs has variance \(2\sigma^2/100\). Its standard deviation is \(\sigma\sqrt{2/100} = 0.08 \times 0.1414 = 0.01131\), which the lab prints as spread_sd_theory. The planted mean spread is \(\beta\) times the mean simulated z-gap the lab prints, \(-0.0008 \times (-2.7937) = 0.0022350\), printed as planted_monthly_spread 0.00223. The z-gap is \(-2.7937\) (200 seeds) rather than the analytic \(-2.7996\) of Proposition 2.3. The lab prints a sweep of the realised gap over firm counts, with 20 seeds each: \(-2.7437\) at 50 firms, \(-2.7728\) at 100, \(-2.7912\) at 500 and \(-2.7991\) at 5,000 (mean_z_gap_firms_50_seeds_20 to mean_z_gap_firms_5000_seeds_20). Their distances from the analytic value, computed as \(-2.7996\) minus each printed value, are 0.0559, 0.0268, 0.0084 and 0.0005. These four printed values are consistent with a finite-sort effect that shrinks as the number of firms sorted each month grows. Four counts with 20 seeds each do not establish the rate of that decay. The 200-seed and 20-seed means at 500 firms differ by 0.0025, which is seed-to-seed variation. The expected \(t\) is \(0.0022350/(0.01131/\sqrt{120}) = 2.1647\) with the displayed SD 0.01131, and \(0.0022350/(0.011314/\sqrt{120}) = 2.164\) with the unrounded SD 0.011314. The lab prints expected_t_at_planted 2.164, computed from unrounded values. The lab's median of 2.155 is close to that value.</p>
