-              <p>The standard error of the 120-month mean spread is \(0.011314/\sqrt{120} = 0.0010328\), using the unrounded theoretical standard deviation. The lab prints the distance computed from unrounded values, with the seed-0 mean and the planted spread at full precision: seed0_distance_in_se \(-1.433\), so the seed-0 draw lies 1.43 standard errors below the planted value. With the rounded seed-0 mean and planted spread (0.00075 and 0.00223) and the unrounded standard error 0.0010328, the quotient is \(-1.433\). With the displayed standard error 0.00103 it is \(-1.4369\), which displays as \(-1.44\). It shows that a single sample of this length can land on the wrong side of the effect, and that a t-statistic of 0.676 is not evidence against the effect. The lab's power of 0.590 summarises the same point across 200 seeds.</p>
