             <span class="problem-title">R1 · Sample length for 80% power</span>
             <p>Using the normal approximation, how many months \(T\) are needed for 80% power at the planted effect, with a two-sided 5% test? Use the same monthly standard deviation as E2.</p>
             <details class="solution"><summary>Solution</summary>
-              <p>Under the normal approximation the test rejects with power \(0.8\) when the mean of the statistic, \(\mu_t = (\text{planted mean}) \times \sqrt{T}/s\), exceeds \(1.96 + 0.8416 = 2.8016\), where \(s = 0.01131\) is the monthly standard deviation and \(0.8416 = \Phi^{-1}(0.8)\). Solving \(0.0022350\sqrt{T}/0.01131 = 2.8016\), with the planted spread \(0.0022350\) of E2, gives \(\sqrt{T} = 14.18\) and \(T \approx 201\) months, about 17 years. The answer is an approximation under the normal reference and an iid assumption, and it treats the planted effect as known. A real study does not know the effect, which is why the power calculation is a design input and not a result.</p>
