         <ul>
           <li><strong>The residual is not a test of intent.</strong> A large discretionary accrual can come from a growing firm whose normal model is misspecified, from an estimate that was honest but wrong, or from deliberate choices. The residual measures a component, and the component is not identified as manipulation. <span class="claim" data-grade="C" data-cite="dechow1995detecting" data-locator="Crossref abstract; passage locator not confirmed">The paper compares the specification and power of commonly used test statistics for detecting earnings management, and no power figure from it is quoted here.</span></li>
-          <li><strong>The receivables adjustment is untested in Lab 1.</strong> The adjustment is a regressor: the change in revenue less the change in receivables, over lagged assets, enters the fit whether or not the manipulation runs through revenue. Lab 1 has no revenue channel, because the planted manipulation enters the discretionary term directly, so the lab cannot test the adjustment.</li>
+          <li><strong>The receivables adjustment is not tested against a revenue manipulation in Lab 1.</strong> The adjustment is a regressor: the change in revenue less the change in receivables, over lagged assets, enters the fit whether or not the manipulation runs through revenue. Lab 1 plants no manipulation through revenue: the planted manipulation enters the discretionary term directly. The revenue-less-receivables term does enter the generator's true accruals, with coefficient 0.10 on (ΔREV − ΔREC)/A, so the lab cannot test whether the adjustment picks up a revenue manipulation.</li>
           <li><strong>Accrual tests see only accruals.</strong> The model of Definition 2.1 has no term for management that works through real activity, such as the timing of discretionary spending or of production. An accrual test is therefore blind to such management by construction. No registry source for that practice is cited in this lesson, so the lesson makes no claim about its size.</li>
           <li><strong>Grouping changes the model.</strong> The model can be estimated within groups of firm-years, such as industry-years, so that industry-wide accrual patterns are absorbed. The A15 contract estimates one cross-section, and Lab 1 has one cross-section, so the lab cannot show the effect of grouping.</li>
         </ul>
