             <span class="problem-title">E3 · How far the planted \(a_2\) is</span>
             <p>In the strong scenario, the non-manipulator estimate of \(a_2\) is 0.103076 against a planted 0.10, and its classical standard error is 0.001467 (both printed by the lab at full precision). Express the difference in standard-error units, compute the two-sided tail probability at that distance, and say whether one seed is enough to judge the regression's recovery.</p>
             <details class="solution"><summary>Solution</summary>
-              <p>The unrounded estimate is \(b = 0.103076\) and the unrounded classical standard error is \(0.001467\), both printed by the lab. The difference from the planted 0.10 is \(0.003076\), so the displayed quotient is \(z = 0.003076/0.001467 = 2.097\) standard errors. The lab's unrounded inputs, which carry more digits, give \(z = 2.096\) (printed as z_a2 2.096). Under correct specification and iid errors, the two-sided tail probability is \(2(1 - \Phi(2.096)) = 0.036\) (lab output two_sided_tail_a2 0.036). Using the rounded inputs 0.0031 and 0.0015 gives \(z = 2.067\) and a tail of 0.039, which is an artefact of rounding the standard error. A draw this far out has that two-sided probability. A single seed cannot settle whether the estimator recovers \(a_2\) without bias. The judgement needs many seeds, which is the design of R1 and not a result of this lesson.</p>
+              <p>The lab prints the estimate \(b = 0.103076\) and the classical standard error \(0.001467\) to six decimals. The difference from the planted 0.10 is \(0.003076\), so the quotient of the six-decimal values is \(z = 0.003076/0.001467 = 2.097\) standard errors. At full precision the lab computes \(z = 2.096\) (printed as z_a2_fp 2.096). Under correct specification and iid errors, the two-sided tail probability is \(2(1 - \Phi(2.096)) = 0.036\) (printed as two_sided_tail_a2_fp 0.036). Using the rounded inputs 0.0031 and 0.0015 gives \(z = 2.067\) and a tail of 0.039. That is an artefact of rounding the inputs: rounding the standard error alone gives \(z = 2.051\) (tail 0.040), and rounding the difference alone gives \(z = 2.113\) (tail 0.035). A draw this far out has that two-sided probability. A single seed cannot settle whether the estimator recovers \(a_2\) without bias. The judgement needs many seeds, which is the design of R1 and not a result of this lesson.</p>
